## 0.5. EXPLICIT THREAT MODEL

This section defines **who attacks, how, and what we protect**.

> **Note:** For detailed threat requirements and numbered rules, see `ocentra-security-rules.mdc` sections 1-15.

### Attacker Capabilities

- Full control over client-side code
- Ability to replay, mutate, or automate any request
- Control over timing (clock skew, delays, retries)
- Ability to generate arbitrary signatures (wrong signer, wrong message)
- Ability to spam transactions (economic exhaustion)
- Ability to exploit partial failures and rollbacks
- Ability to forge internal headers and bypass gateways
- Ability to manipulate serialization and encoding

### Attacker Goals

- Extract value (credits, rewards, assets)
- Cause economic exhaustion (system cost > attacker cost)
- Exploit partial failures for profit
- Bypass security controls (auth, rate limits, kill switches)
- Cause inconsistent state (split-brain, rollback abuse)
- Exploit time-based logic (cooldowns, expiry, epochs)

### Non-Goals (Out of Scope)

- Physical infrastructure attacks
- Social engineering
- Supply chain attacks on dependencies (handled separately)
- Zero-day exploits in underlying platforms

### Assumed Trusted Components (Minimal)

- Solana validator network (finality assumptions)
- Cryptographic primitives (Ed25519, SHA-256)
- Hardware security modules (if used for backend signing)

### Explicitly Rejected Assumptions

- Client clock is accurate (see Rule 0.1.2 in `ocentra-security-rules.mdc`)
- Client randomness is unpredictable
- Network latency is bounded
- RPC nodes are honest
- Internal services are trusted (see Rule 0.1.5 in `ocentra-security-rules.mdc`)
- Error handlers are safe
- Rollback logic is correct by default (see Rule 0.1.4 in `ocentra-security-rules.mdc`)
- Time-based logic is safe by default (see Rule 0.1.2 in `ocentra-security-rules.mdc`)
- Administrative actions are inherently safe (see Rule 0.1.5 in `ocentra-security-rules.mdc`)

### Threat → Test Mapping Requirement

Every threat MUST map to:

- At least one property-based test
- At least one integration test
- At least one fuzz test (where applicable)

**Rule**

> If a threat cannot be mapped to tests, the threat model is incomplete.

For specific threat requirements, see `ocentra-security-rules.mdc` sections 1-15.

